## Decide what must survive

An environment contains several kinds of state: source files, installed software, configuration, and application data. A snapshot may cover some of these while a database or external storage covers others. Read the scope before treating a snapshot as a complete backup.

## Save a recovery point

Use a descriptive name and record the source resource. A useful recovery record identifies the saved version and explains when you would restore it.

## Restore and inspect

After restoring, verify the files, configuration and application behavior that matter to the task. A successful restore response is not a substitute for checking the application.

## Snapshot or volume?

| Need | Useful starting point |
| --- | --- |
| Recreate an environment at a known state | Snapshot |
| Keep changing files across process restarts | Persistent volume |
| Query application records | Database |
| Store exported artifacts | Object storage |

## Practice recovery

In the [sandbox lab](/docs/learn/labs/sandbox), create a machine, save a snapshot, remove the machine and restore it. The lab makes the distinction between an active instance and a saved state visible.
